{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"7da24f833c54df14","type":"group","x":3140,"y":-1412,"width":2740,"height":932,"label":"Comparator of Servomechanism Function (Microscopic Level))"},
		{"id":"dca6083e3543fc83","type":"group","x":2631,"y":5540,"width":1769,"height":830,"label":"Summary of Connections"},
		{"id":"689f668dd28b8fae","type":"group","x":2760,"y":480,"width":1820,"height":760,"label":"Comparator of Servomechanism Function (Macroscopic Model)"},
		{"id":"fae7f0f84ca31029","type":"group","x":4220,"y":-1340,"width":1597,"height":480,"label":"Feed-forward Inhibition"},
		{"id":"682b6a82511430b5","type":"group","x":3160,"y":-1340,"width":1030,"height":618,"label":"Correction of Erroneus Movement"},
		{"id":"cfb20b6b9813d874","type":"text","text":"### Internal Circuitry","x":600,"y":-719,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"c49c93a67a377d31","type":"text","text":"- **Climbing Fibres:**\n\t- Climbing Fibres arise from Inf. Olive.\n\t- 1 Climbing Fibre synapses w Nuclear Cell & only 1 PC.\n\t- These fibres twine around the PC and can make upto 300 synapses. When stimulated, a prolonged complex spike in seen instead of a regular AP.\n- **Mossy Fibres:**\n\t- Input from any other part of body than Inf. Olive is carried by **Mossy Fibres**. These supply GRs.\n\t- 1 Mossy fibre supplies multiple neurons.","x":1280,"y":-1440,"width":568,"height":293},
		{"id":"b93f690c1311d610","type":"text","text":"![[Pasted image 20251018190109.png]]","x":1362,"y":-1050,"width":1198,"height":730},
		{"id":"832e0f5086ff9452","type":"text","text":"# CEREBELLUM","x":-120,"y":-2360,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"065435bbcee17ff8","type":"text","text":"## Learning Objectives\n##### After this course, you will be able to-\n- Describe the Gross Anatomy and Microcircuitry of the cerebellum \n- Divide the Cerebellum into different parts from functional and evolutionary perspectives.\n- Describe the Connections of the cerebellum with other structures of the CNS (Central Nervous System).\n- Describe the Functions of the cerebellum and the electrical circuits and mechanisms behind them.\n- Describe in details the role of the cerebellum in co-ordination and error correction of movement.\n##### Coming soon\n- Cerebellum and Timing Mechanisms\n- Forward Models in the Cerebellum\n","x":-1780,"y":-2516,"width":640,"height":372},
		{"id":"9537c08e025af440","type":"text","text":"Latin for little brain. \n","x":-1200,"y":-440,"width":320,"height":60},
		{"id":"0d2c3baddaf4648d","type":"text","text":"Located posterior to pons.\n","x":-1200,"y":-360,"width":320,"height":60},
		{"id":"e1f3558ab1d0b66c","type":"text","text":"### Connections","x":871,"y":2558,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"ede2011bd4c0a028","type":"text","text":"![[image-4.png]]","x":71,"y":3195,"width":1324,"height":765},
		{"id":"2c8fec599dfae7fb","type":"text","text":"1. Granule Cells (GR) - Only excitatory cell. Stimulates all other cells.\n2. Golgi Cells (GC)\n3. Basket Cells (BC)\n4. Stellate Cells (SC)\n5. Purkinje Cells (PC) - Largest\n","x":1180,"y":-1760,"width":364,"height":200},
		{"id":"f04eb1ef72dd7164","type":"text","text":"Connected to the pons via Supeior, Middle and Inferior Cerebellar Peduncles.\n","x":-1200,"y":-280,"width":320,"height":77},
		{"id":"b0677cea2570b06b","type":"text","text":"#### Other Functions\n1. Regulation of muscle tone & deep tendon reflexes.\n2. Maintenance of Posture & Equilibrium by Flocculonodular Node <- Vestigial\n3. Movement of the eye (Flocculonodular Node)\n","x":1078,"y":580,"width":408,"height":180},
		{"id":"beae5c59797bdfe5","type":"text","text":"### Functions","x":1202,"y":360,"width":160,"height":50},
		{"id":"a31d0dc46818fe8e","type":"text","text":"Detected by Inf. Olive","x":3360,"y":-1168,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"cf4d3662bf5ee0c9","type":"text","text":"Nuclear Cells \n(Responsible for Output)","x":3551,"y":-992,"width":250,"height":68,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"007ff9967b669164","type":"text","text":"Wrong Movement","x":3360,"y":-1290,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"a469594b0bc843df","type":"text","text":"Stellate Cells","x":5037,"y":-1320,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b32a02ca62aa5e86","type":"text","text":"# Normal Physiology","x":-145,"y":-240,"width":300,"height":60},
		{"id":"a282640f231dae76","type":"text","text":"### Anatomy","x":-1973,"y":350,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e06f41b111cd0471","type":"text","text":"![[image-13.png]]","x":-3940,"y":200,"width":1196,"height":640},
		{"id":"fce891da52c9895f","type":"text","text":"![[image-9.png]]","x":-3844,"y":1360,"width":824,"height":560},
		{"id":"a738bd741856479c","type":"text","text":"Effector Organ","x":2780,"y":909,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"1c9b81b4df6de8ae","type":"text","text":"### Divisions","x":-740,"y":1440,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e8eb2c1198ca190a","type":"text","text":"![[image-11.png]]","x":2591,"y":1730,"width":1000,"height":1000},
		{"id":"7302a5374fda709f","type":"text","text":"![[image-1.png]]","x":-2560,"y":2560,"width":1215,"height":597},
		{"id":"4f3bde24bfe2b020","type":"text","text":"| Functional Divisions                           | Evolutionary History | Anatomical Parts                  | Projects to                    | Function                                                                   | Nuclei                         | Function (approx.)                          |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------- |\n| **Cerebrocerebellum** / **Neocerebellum**      | Newest               | Lateral Part of Cortex            | Motor & Premotor Cortices      | Motor Planning                                                             | Dentate                        | Smooth execution of movement with precision |\n| **Spinocerebellum** / **Paleocerebellum**      | Intermediate         | Vermis & Intermed. zone of Cortex | Medial & Lateral Desc. Tracts  | Motor Execution                                                            | Fastigial, Emboliform, Globose | Appendicular stability                      |\n| **Vestibulocerebellum** / **Arechecerebellum** | Oldest               | Flocculonodular Lobe              | Vestibular Nuclei of Brainstem | Balance & Eye movement (More dominant in lower animals, reduced in humans) | Fastigial                      | Truncal stability (Balance)                 |","x":-2300,"y":3400,"width":1122,"height":300},
		{"id":"e352672b6d85fea5","type":"text","text":"![[Connections of Cerebellum Flowchart.png]]","x":2651,"y":5570,"width":1729,"height":780},
		{"id":"07d258df6de4b339","type":"text","text":"Complex Spike d/t 200-300 synapses","x":3182,"y":-802,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"270b97a1ef644c88","type":"text","text":"Purkinje Cell","x":3180,"y":-988,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"480e523a9540bff0","type":"text","text":"### Comparator of Servomechanism Function of Cerebellum","x":1918,"y":357,"width":562,"height":53},
		{"id":"eae7b6119227d013","type":"text","text":"Cortex","x":3201,"y":500,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"a7b03b89a037dc4d","type":"text","text":"Cerebellum","x":3660,"y":729,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"630ccf9a66487589","type":"text","text":"Error Correction via Internal Circuitry \n$$Error = \\text{Movement Executed} - \\text{Intended Movement by Motor Cortex}$$\nError is corrected and output is relayed","x":3805,"y":1089,"width":760,"height":131},
		{"id":"9907e2386c5f3fde","type":"text","text":"![[Efferent Connections of Cerebellum.png]]","x":1767,"y":3075,"width":1073,"height":925},
		{"id":"bfeb8d76736e7859","type":"text","text":"Suppression of Erroneous movement","x":3920,"y":-802,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"47364cb9e2de29b2","type":"text","text":"Long Term Depression","x":3551,"y":-562,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"bf79e9ccddf7fce9","type":"text","text":"Inhibition of Wrong Movement","x":4203,"y":-563,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"9e28b4d19cb3cba4","type":"text","text":"# Role of Cerebellum in Coordination","x":5319,"y":-578,"width":460,"height":75,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"890a3e22946579c2","type":"text","text":"Indirect stimulation of Correct Movement","x":4895,"y":-740,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"102e0e07c9af7597","type":"text","text":"Output","x":5483,"y":-960,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"bc4c96b57b12646d","type":"text","text":"Deep Cerebellar Nuclei","x":5037,"y":-960,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0fdf0d34bc39018b","type":"text","text":"Grey Matter other than Inf. Olive project Mossy Fibres","x":4257,"y":-1140,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"c92a6398ee23444c","type":"text","text":"Purkinje Cells","x":5037,"y":-1080,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"37a45a6ce560ff01","type":"text","text":"Early inhibition prevents excessive Purkinje firing → avoids undue suppression of nuclear activity → ensures **smooth, uninterrupted movement flow**.","x":5437,"y":-1238,"width":342,"height":137},
		{"id":"10926320ce99da9b","type":"text","text":"Granule Cells","x":4637,"y":-1200,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"f98549c21585604e","type":"text","text":"Basket Cells","x":5037,"y":-1200,"width":250,"height":60}
	],
